%description:

Tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber:clock -- the ISS comes from the clock, one tick in 4 microseconds.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "-1: from the clock, one tick in 4 microseconds (RFC 9293 section
3.4.1)".

The classes: clock (-1, the default), fixed (a value from 0 to 4294967295), random (a
distribution, which the parameter takes again for each connection, because it is volatile),
invalid (below -1 or above 4294967295). The code: TcpConnection::selectInitialSeqNum(). This test separates "clock" from "fixed" and "random": their
ISS does not follow the time of the SYN.

Host under test: host1, the active opener. Observation: level 1, the sequence number of the
SYN of host1 is the time of the SYN times 250000, modulo 2^32.

%# parameter: Tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber
%# class: clock
%# value: -1
%# key: *.host1.tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpInitialSendSequenceNumberClock")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .first()
                  .assertEvent([](const MatchContext& context) { return (uint32_t)evalPacketField(context.event.packet, "tcp.sequenceNo").intValue() == (uint32_t)(unsigned long)std::fmod(context.event.time.dbl() * 250000.0, 4294967296.0); })
                  .describe("the SYN of host1, with the ISS of the clock")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpInitialSendSequenceNumberClock compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.initialSendSequenceNumber = -1    # the parameter under test
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpInitialSendSequenceNumberClock: PASS
